1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,677
Learn how to find a clinical trial near you, and how to read a study page. No account needed.

2
00:00:08,877 --> 00:00:17,649
This is ClinicalTrials.gov, a free public list of medical research studies, run by the U.S. National Library of Medicine.

3
00:00:19,399 --> 00:00:26,763
In the first box, Condition or disease, type the health condition you want to learn about. We'll type type 2 diabetes.

4
00:00:28,513 --> 00:00:33,631
Next, in the Location box, start typing the city where you live. We'll use Boston.

5
00:00:35,381 --> 00:00:41,833
A list of places appears. Choose Boston, Massachusetts, so the site knows exactly which place you mean.

6
00:00:43,583 --> 00:00:45,263
Now press the blue Search button.

7
00:00:50,471 --> 00:00:59,069
The site found 299 studies about type 2 diabetes with a location in Boston. But many of them are already finished.

8
00:01:00,819 --> 00:01:07,833
For example, this first study says Completed, no longer looking for participants. You can't join a study like this.

9
00:01:09,583 --> 00:01:15,438
On the left, find Study Status. Recruiting means the study is looking for volunteers right now.

10
00:01:17,188 --> 00:01:18,797
Tick the box next to Recruiting.

11
00:01:21,229 --> 00:01:24,174
Then press Apply Filters at the bottom of the panel.

12
00:01:25,924 --> 00:01:30,710
Now the list shows 28 studies, and every one is marked Recruiting, in green.

13
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:38,467
Let's open one. Click the study title to see its full page.

14
00:01:40,217 --> 00:01:45,444
At the top you see the study's title, and on the right, its status. This one is Recruiting.

15
00:01:47,194 --> 00:01:59,579
Scroll down to Study Overview. Intervention or Treatment tells you what is being tested. Here it's two diabetes pills, elecoglipron and dapagliflozin, alone and together.

16
00:02:01,329 --> 00:02:09,194
Some groups get a placebo. A placebo looks like the real medicine, but has no medicine in it, so researchers can compare fairly.

17
00:02:10,944 --> 00:02:20,618
Phase tells you how far along the testing is. Phase 3 means the treatment has already been tested in smaller groups, and is now being compared in many more people.

18
00:02:22,368 --> 00:02:30,986
Next, Participation Criteria. Eligibility criteria are the rules for who can join a study, such as age and health history.

19
00:02:32,736 --> 00:02:40,554
Inclusion criteria are things you must have. For example, a type 2 diabetes diagnosis for at least 90 days.

20
00:02:42,304 --> 00:02:49,909
Exclusion criteria are things that would keep you out. For example, people with type 1 diabetes can't join this study.

21
00:02:51,659 --> 00:02:58,413
Below the list, you can see the ages that can join, here 18 years and older, and that all sexes are welcome.

22
00:03:00,163 --> 00:03:07,808
To ask about joining, go to Contacts and Locations. This study lists a contact phone number and email for questions.

23
00:03:09,558 --> 00:03:19,878
Below that is every place the study runs. Find your state. Here, the two Boston sites say Not yet recruiting, so call the contact to ask when they will open.

24
00:03:21,628 --> 00:03:31,768
One important note. A listing here is not medical advice, and the government does not review every study. Before you join any study, talk with your doctor.

25
00:03:35,918 --> 00:03:39,177
Let's try the same steps once more, for high blood pressure.

26
00:03:40,927 --> 00:03:43,758
This time, type Chicago in the Location box.

27
00:03:45,508 --> 00:03:47,746
Choose Chicago, Illinois from the list.

28
00:03:49,994 --> 00:03:50,908
And press Search.

29
00:03:54,124 --> 00:03:58,569
There are 348 studies, but again, many are already finished.

30
00:04:00,319 --> 00:04:01,698
So tick Recruiting again,

31
00:04:03,995 --> 00:04:05,178
and apply the filter.

32
00:04:07,706 --> 00:04:14,902
Now 32 studies are left, all looking for volunteers. From here, open any title and read it the same way.

33
00:04:16,552 --> 00:04:30,291
To recap. Search for a condition near you. Keep only recruiting studies. Open a study to see what's being tested, who can join, where it runs, and who to contact. And always talk with your doctor before joining.
